# What Vercel is allowed to see.
#
# This used to be "the tracker and nothing else", because the collector ran on a
# machine at home and a datacentre was the wrong place for a scanner. That is no
# longer the arrangement: Vercel runs the collector too, so the Python package
# it runs has to go up with it.
#
# Written as a denylist of everything with the four things carved back out,
# rather than a list of things to leave behind, because the failure that matters
# is the one nobody thought to name. A .env with live Broadcastify credentials
# in it is exactly the file an ignore list forgets, and this way it is never a
# question -- it is not named, so it does not go.
/*

# The front end's source, and the functions that serve it.
!/web
!/api

# The collector. api/collect.py imports this package directly rather than
# vendoring a copy of it, which is the whole reason the deployment root is the
# repository root: one parser, one gazetteer, one set of thresholds, exercised
# by the CLI and by the deployment both. A copy would be the thing that quietly
# stops matching the tests.
!/firewall

# And what the build itself needs to read.
!/vercel.json

# There is deliberately no requirements.txt, and no pyproject.toml either --
# the packaging lives in setup.cfg, which explains itself at length.
#
# The collector installs nothing: it reaches Broadcastify, the transcription API
# and Redis over urllib, signs its own JWT with hmac, and every third-party
# import left in firewall/ -- requests, faster_whisper, anthropic -- sits inside
# a function body the hosted path never calls. So a cold start is the runtime
# and the source, with no wheel to fetch and no version that can break because
# something upstream shipped on a Tuesday.
#
# Keeping them out matters more than it looks. Vercel's generic Python preset
# matches on filename alone -- requirements.txt, pyproject.toml or Pipfile in
# the build root -- and matching it would end this deployment: it forces the
# Python builder and takes precedence over file-based functions, so every route
# would go to one WSGI application that does not exist here and nothing under
# api/ would be built at all. The denylist above already does that job, since
# nothing is carved back in for them. setup.cfg says the rest.
#
# Two things this file cannot reach, both project settings, both documented in
# api/README.md because both fail quietly: a Framework Preset of Python saved
# against the project, which outlives whatever file first caused it, and a Root
# Directory of `web`, left from when the tracker was the whole deployment. From
# inside web/ there is no vercel.json, no api/ and no firewall package -- the
# build succeeds and publishes nothing.

# The two things inside web/ that are made, not written.
/web/node_modules
/web/dist

# Never, under any circumstance, and stated again here rather than relied upon
# from the denylist above. These hold the real Broadcastify key.
.env
.env.*
